WEBVTT

00:00:00.103 --> 00:00:02.221
Let us begin with a basic question

00:00:03.158 --> 00:00:04.816
Why do we need to organize code

00:00:04.842 --> 00:00:07.553
at all For a very small script

00:00:07.803 --> 00:00:11.132
organization may not seem important If your

00:00:11.158 --> 00:00:13.053
program is only ten or twenty lines

00:00:13.066 --> 00:00:15.197
long putting everything in one file may

00:00:15.224 --> 00:00:19.066
be perfectly fine But analytics projects often

00:00:19.079 --> 00:00:21.618
grow You may start with a few

00:00:21.645 --> 00:00:25.184
calculations then add data cleaning summary
statistics

00:00:25.342 --> 00:00:28.737
visualizations helper functions reports and perhaps
a

00:00:28.750 --> 00:00:32.250
predictive model Over time one file can

00:00:32.263 --> 00:00:34.447
become hundreds or even thousands of lines

00:00:34.461 --> 00:00:37.184
long When everything is stored in one

00:00:37.211 --> 00:00:40.789
place several problems can appear It becomes

00:00:40.816 --> 00:00:42.132
harder to find the part of the

00:00:42.158 --> 00:00:44.947
program you need A change in one

00:00:44.974 --> 00:00:49.039
area may accidentally affect another area Repeated

00:00:49.066 --> 00:00:52.382
code may appear in several places Team

00:00:52.408 --> 00:00:55.237
members may have difficulty understanding which
section

00:00:55.263 --> 00:00:58.987
is responsible for which task Organized code

00:00:59.013 --> 00:01:01.987
gives related work a clear home For

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:04.868
example financial calculations can live in one

00:01:04.895 --> 00:01:08.605
module Data-cleaning functions can live in another

00:01:08.618 --> 00:01:12.092
module A class that represents a customer

00:01:12.118 --> 00:01:15.368
can live in another file This organization

00:01:15.395 --> 00:01:17.276
is useful for teams but it is

00:01:17.303 --> 00:01:20.513
also useful when you work alone Imagine

00:01:20.539 --> 00:01:22.776
returning to a project three months later

00:01:23.697 --> 00:01:25.868
A clean structure helps you understand your

00:01:25.895 --> 00:01:28.632
own work much more quickly So when

00:01:28.658 --> 00:01:31.355
we talk about modules packages and classes

00:01:31.592 --> 00:01:33.539
we are not adding complexity just for

00:01:33.553 --> 00:01:36.513
the sake of complexity We are using

00:01:36.539 --> 00:01:38.921
structure to make larger programs easier to

00:01:38.947 --> 00:01:41.763
understand reuse test and maintain
